<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#criminal-tribes-act">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Criminal Tribes Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as CTA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Criminal Tribes Act was the name given to a series of colonial laws in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, from 1871 to 1924, that criminalised entire communities by designating them and their members as habitual criminals. Once notified, a community had no legal recourse, and its members were bound to compulsory registration and a pass system that limited where they could travel and live. At independence in 1947 thirteen million people in 127 communities were subject to it. The act was repealed in August 1949 and the communities denotified in 1952, but the Habitual Offenders Act that replaced it re-stigmatised them, and the denotified tribes still face its legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/criminal-tribes-act.jpg" alt="Criminal Tribes Act" width="330" height="523" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Pamphlet_on_Gobinda_Doms_Gang,_under_the_Criminal_Tribes_Act_(VI_of_1924),_dated_1942.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1881 the census ranked thousands of jatis by their supposed purity, administrative jobs went only to the upper castes, and the <b>Criminal Tribes Act</b> of 1871 declared whole communities criminal by birth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
